        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Treadmills rarely fail their owners by breaking. They fail by being the wrong machine — a
          deck that is too short for the person running on it, a motor rated for a number that
          does not matter, a frame that will not go up the stairs. Each of the mistakes below is
          common, each is avoidable with a tape measure or ten minutes of reading, and each links
          to the page that covers it properly.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Short Answer: Fifteen Treadmill Buying Mistakes</h2>
<p>
            The mistakes that cost buyers most, roughly in order of how often we see them:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Comparing motors on peak horsepower</strong> rather than continuous (CHP).</li>
            <li><strong>Buying a deck too short</strong> for the longest stride in the house.</li>
            <li><strong>Ignoring ceiling height</strong> and the clearance needed behind the belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Forgetting the delivery route</strong> — doors, stair turns, bannisters.</li>
            <li><strong>Choosing a weight capacity</strong> with no headroom above the heaviest user.</li>
            <li><strong>Signing up to subscription lock-in</strong> without checking what the console does without it.</li>
            <li><strong>Reading only the frame warranty</strong> and not the labour term.</li>
            <li><strong>Never standing on one</strong>, or not knowing the return terms.</li>
            <li><strong>Buying for the runner you hope to become</strong> rather than the walker you are.</li>
            <li><strong>Paying for a fold you will never use.</strong></li>
            <li><strong>Putting it straight on carpet</strong> with no mat.</li>
            <li><strong>Running it from an extension cord</strong> or a crowded circuit.</li>
            <li><strong>Buying the wrong format</strong> — a walking pad for running, or a full machine for a desk.</li>
            <li><strong>Forgetting running costs</strong> such as lubricant, belts and service.</li>
            <li><strong>Buying used without an inspection.</strong></li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you only have time to act on three, measure the room including ceiling height,
            measure the delivery route, and compare continuous motor ratings. Those three account
            for most of the machines that end up resold within a year. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-buying-guide-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill buying guide</a>
            covers the decision from the beginning; this page is the list of ways it tends to go
            wrong.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="peak-horsepower" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Mistake 1: Comparing Motors on Peak Horsepower</h2>
<p>
            The largest number on a treadmill box is usually the peak horsepower, and it is the
            least useful number on it. Peak is the momentary output a motor can hit for a second
            or two, typically at start-up. Continuous horsepower — CHP — is what it can sustain
            without overheating, and continuous output is what you actually ask of a motor during
            a forty-minute walk.
          </p>
          <p>
            Peak figures typically run 1.5 to 2 times the continuous rating, so a motor sold as
            "4.0 HP peak" may only sustain around 2.0 to 2.5. That is a walking motor. The buyer
            who compares a 4.0 peak machine with a 3.0 CHP machine and picks the bigger number has
            usually bought the weaker motor. If a listing does not say "continuous" anywhere, assume
            it is quoting peak and treat the figure with suspicion.
          </p>
          <p>
            As a broad guide, walking needs around 2.0 to 2.5 CHP, regular jogging around 2.75 to
            3.0, and daily running 3.5 or more, with a little extra for heavier users. An
            underpowered motor does not usually fail dramatically. It runs hot, the belt hesitates
            under your footstrike, and the controller board beside it ages faster. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            has the full table by weight and pace.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-too-short" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Mistake 2: Buying a Deck Too Short for Your Stride</h2>
<p>
            Deck length is set by stride, and stride is set far more by pace than by height. A
            walker covers much less belt per step than the same person running, which is why a deck
            that feels roomy in a showroom at walking pace can feel cramped the first time you
            push the speed up at home.
          </p>
          <p>
            The symptom of a short deck is rarely falling off the back. It is unconsciously
            shortening your stride to stay away from the motor hood at the front, which changes
            your gait and makes running on the machine feel like a compromise. People in that
            position tend to stop running on it without quite knowing why.
          </p>
          <p>
            The typical guidance is 50 to 55 inches for walking, 55 inches for jogging if you are
            under about 5 foot 10, and 60 inches for running or for anyone taller, with width
            moving from 18 to 20 or 22 inches as pace rises. Buy for the longest stride in the
            household, not the average. The
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            breaks this down by height and pace, and explains why buying more deck than anyone
            needs is also a mistake — it costs floor space, weight and motor effort for nothing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-and-delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Mistakes 3 and 4: Ignoring Ceiling Height, and Forgetting the Delivery Route</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Ceiling height.</strong> You do not run at your own height on a treadmill. You
            run at your height plus the deck height, and decks sit anywhere from about 5 inches
            off the floor on a walking pad to 12 inches or more on a cushioned home machine. Add
            the vertical bounce of running and at least 12 inches of head clearance, and a tall
            runner on a typical machine can need close to eight feet of ceiling. Basements with
            low beams and ducts are where this mistake usually happens, and a machine with
            incline raises the front further still.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Clearance.</strong> The footprint on the spec sheet is the machine, not the
            space it needs. The usual safety guidance is about 6 feet of clear floor behind the
            belt and 2 feet either side, which turns an 80 by 36 inch machine into something nearer
            a 12 by 7 foot installation. The clearance behind is not a comfort margin; it is the
            distance you travel if you lose your footing. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space requirements guide</a>
            has the formula for ceiling height and the full clearance rules.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The delivery route.</strong> A treadmill has to get from the kerb to the room,
            and that journey is where a surprising number of purchases stall. Full-size machines are
            often 36 inches or more across the rails, which will not pass a standard interior door
            square-on, and the box is larger than the machine. Measure every doorway, every stair
            turn, the height under any landing and the clearance past bannisters — the narrowest
            point decides, not the average.
          </p>
          <p>
            Weight matters too. Folding home machines typically weigh 150 to 250 lb assembled and
            non-folding ones more, and the boxed weight is higher still. Find out whether delivery
            means kerbside, room of choice or full assembly before you order, because "free
            delivery" usually means the first of those; our
            <a href="/treadmill-delivery-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill delivery guide</a>
            explains the service levels. If it will need carrying up a flight of
            stairs, read
            <a href="/how-to-move-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to move a treadmill</a>
            before the lorry arrives rather than after.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Mistake 5: Choosing a Weight Capacity With No Headroom</h2>
<p>
            The maximum user weight on a spec sheet is a warranty boundary, not a comfort rating.
            Every running stride loads the deck with a multiple of body weight, and a machine
            operating at its rated limit is operating at its design limit on every step — the
            motor, rollers, deck and frame all working harder than they would with margin to
            spare.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rule of thumb we use across the site is to buy a capacity roughly 50 lb above the
            heaviest regular user, and a little more for runners. If the printed figure only just
            covers you, a small change in weight takes you outside the warranty terms too. Weight
            capacity is also a useful proxy for build: a manufacturer that rates a machine
            generously usually did so because the frame, rollers and motor can support it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The households that get this wrong are often buying for one person and then sharing
            the machine with a heavier partner or a teenager who has outgrown the original plan.
            Size the machine for the heaviest person who will use it regularly. The
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a>
            works through the arithmetic, and our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">heavy-user roundup</a>
            covers machines built with that margin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="subscription-lock-in" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Mistake 6: Subscription Lock-In You Did Not Price</h2>
<p>
            Many mid-range and premium treadmills are now designed around a training platform —
            iFIT on NordicTrack and ProForm machines, for example — and the touchscreen,
            trainer-led classes and automatic incline control are the selling point. That is fine
            if you want it and have priced it. It becomes a mistake when you discover after
            delivery how much of the console depends on an active membership.
          </p>
          <p>
            The question to ask before buying is simple: what does this console do with no
            subscription active? On some machines the answer is "everything that matters, plus a
            manual mode". On others, programmes, tracking or even comfortable access to basic
            controls are routed through the app, and the free trial included with the machine is
            the start of a recurring cost rather than a bonus. Over the life of a treadmill that
            cost can approach the price of the hardware.
          </p>
          <p>
            Price the membership over five years, not one, and decide whether you would still buy
            the machine if you cancelled it in year two. Our
            <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT guide</a> covers
            when the subscription earns its keep, and
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            covers the machines that work fully without one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-labour" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Mistake 7: Reading the Frame Warranty and Nothing Else</h2>
<p>
            "Lifetime warranty" in large type usually refers to the frame, which is the part of a
            treadmill least likely to fail. The terms that decide what a repair costs you are the
            ones in smaller type: the motor, the parts — which on most machines means the
            controller board, the incline motor and the electronics — and labour.
          </p>
          <p>
            Labour is the term buyers skip most often and regret most often. A warranty that
            supplies a replacement controller board free but leaves you to fit it, or to pay a
            technician's call-out to do so, is a much thinner promise than its headline suggests.
            One or two years of in-home labour is typical on mid-range machines; ninety days or
            none at all is not unusual at the budget end. Check also whether parts are shipped to
            you or fitted by a technician, whether the warranty requires registration within a set
            period, and whether wear items such as the belt and deck are excluded.
          </p>
          <p>
            A machine with a ten-year frame, a long motor term and two years of in-home labour is
            usually a better-protected purchase than one advertising a lifetime frame with one year
            of parts and no labour. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains each tier and what tends to void cover.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="no-test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Mistake 8: Never Standing on One, and Not Reading the Return Terms</h2>
<p>
            Most treadmills are now bought online, often without the buyer having stood on the
            model or anything like it. That is not unreasonable — few showrooms stock a wide range
            — but it means the deck feel, the noise at your pace, the reach to the controls and the
            height of the step-up are all things you discover at home, after assembly.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where you can, try something comparable: the same brand's adjacent model in a store,
            a similar deck size at a gym, or a friend's machine. Walk and jog on it at the speeds
            you will actually use. Notice whether the console is within easy reach without
            leaning, whether the handrails are where your hands want them, and whether the deck
            feels stable or bouncy when you push off.
          </p>
          <p>
            Where you cannot, the return policy becomes the test. Find out before ordering how
            long the return window is, whether it applies once the machine has been assembled, who
            arranges and pays for collection, and whether there is a restocking fee. Those terms
            vary widely between retailers selling the same machine, and on a heavy item the cost of
            return freight can be large enough to make a "returnable" machine effectively
            non-returnable. Buy from the retailer whose terms you can live with if the machine is
            wrong; our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill return policies</a>
            covers what to check.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="aspiration-and-folding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Mistakes 9 and 10: Buying for Aspirations, and Folding When You Will Not Fold</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buying for the runner you might become.</strong> A common pattern is a walker
            who buys a 60-inch, 3.5 CHP running machine because they intend to start running. Some
            do, and are glad of it. Many keep walking, and have paid for a larger, heavier, more
            expensive machine than their use needs — money that could have gone into a better
            deck, a longer warranty or simply staying in budget.
          </p>
          <p>
            The honest test is what you have done consistently over the past few months, not what
            you plan to do next. If you walk now, buy a very good walking machine, perhaps with
            strong incline, and upgrade if running becomes a habit. The reverse mistake also
            exists — a regular runner talked into a budget machine — and our pages on
            <a href="/how-much-should-you-spend-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how much to spend</a>
            and <a href="/cheap-vs-premium-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cheap versus premium</a>
            cover where the money makes a difference you can feel.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Paying for a fold you will never use.</strong> Folding is genuinely useful in a
            room that has to be something else most of the time. But a fold costs something: the
            hinge is a wear point, folding frames tend to be less rigid than fixed ones at the same
            price, and a folded machine still stands five or six feet tall on roughly a three by
            three foot patch of floor. If the treadmill will live in a spare room or garage and stay
            down, a non-folding machine usually gives you a stiffer frame and a better deck for the
            same money. The
            <a href="/folding-vs-non-folding-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding versus non-folding comparison</a>
            covers the trade in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="floor-and-power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Mistakes 11 and 12: No Mat on Carpet, and the Wrong Power Supply</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Putting it straight on carpet.</strong> A treadmill on bare carpet draws fibre
            and dust into the motor compartment through the air intake, sits less stably, and
            leaves crush marks that do not recover. Carpet fibre in the motor compartment is a
            contributor to the heat-related electronic failures that are among the more expensive
            repairs a treadmill needs. A proper treadmill mat, larger than the machine's footprint,
            helps with all three. On hard floors it is more often optional, though it still
            protects the floor and helps a little with structure-borne noise in flats. Our guides on
            <a href="/do-you-need-a-treadmill-mat/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether you need a treadmill mat</a>
            and <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills on carpet</a>
            cover materials and thickness.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Running it from an extension cord or a crowded circuit.</strong> Most treadmill
            manuals specify a grounded wall outlet and prohibit extension cords and ordinary power
            strips, because a motor's sustained draw heats the cord and its connections and drops
            voltage at the machine. Some manufacturers recommend or require a dedicated circuit.
            Plan the location around an outlet, check what else shares that circuit, and if the
            machine cannot reach one, have a licensed electrician add an outlet rather than
            improvising. A machine that repeatedly
            <a href="/treadmill-trips-breaker/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">trips the breaker</a>
            is usually telling you about the circuit, the machine, or both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wrong-format" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Mistake 13: Buying the Wrong Format for the Job</h2>
<p>
            "Treadmill" now covers several different machines, and buying the wrong one is a
            category mistake rather than a specification one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A walking pad for running.</strong> Under-desk walking pads are built for slow,
            steady walking, often with a short deck, a low top speed, no handrail and a compact
            motor. They are excellent at that job. They are not small treadmills, and running on
            one is uncomfortable at best. Our
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad versus treadmill comparison</a>
            sets out where the line falls.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A full treadmill for a desk.</strong> The reverse problem: a conventional
            treadmill's console and handrails get in the way of a desk, and its motor is sized for
            exercise sessions rather than many hours at a slow walk. If the goal is walking while
            you work, look at the
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk category</a>
            instead.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A touchscreen instead of hardware.</strong> At a fixed budget, money spent on a
            large screen is money not spent on the motor, rollers and deck. If you will watch your
            own tablet or television anyway, a machine with a simple console and better internals
            is usually the stronger buy. And if the goal is low-impact cardio rather than walking
            or running specifically, it is worth reading
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill versus elliptical</a>
            before assuming a treadmill is the answer at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-costs-and-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Mistakes 14 and 15: Forgetting Running Costs, and Buying Used Blind</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Forgetting the running costs.</strong> A treadmill is not a buy-once purchase.
            It needs silicone lubricant on most decks every 40 to 50 hours of use, periodic belt
            tension and tracking checks, an occasional vacuum of the motor compartment, and — over
            a long ownership — a replacement belt and possibly a drive belt or motor brushes. Add
            any subscription, a mat, and the electricity. None of it is large individually, but it
            belongs in the budget alongside the purchase price. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance cost guide</a>
            itemises the typical year, and the
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance guide</a>
            covers what to actually do.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buying used without an inspection.</strong> A used treadmill can be excellent
            value, particularly a well-built machine that was barely used. But manufacturer
            warranties are typically non-transferable, so a private sale usually comes with no
            cover at all, and the problems that matter — a worn deck, a slipping or delaminating
            belt, a tired motor, a controller board on the way out — are not visible in a photo. Run
            it at your speed, listen to it, look under the belt, and walk away from a seller who will
            not let you. The
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            is the inspection to take with you, and
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new versus used</a>
            covers whether the saving is worth the risk.
          </p>
          <p>
            A related timing note: treadmill pricing does move through the year, and buying in a
            hurry at full price is a mild version of the same mistake. If you have flexibility,
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">when to buy</a>
            covers the patterns. Do not, however, let a sale talk you into a machine that fails any
            of the checks above — a discount on the wrong treadmill is not a saving.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">13. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the biggest mistakes when buying a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The costliest are comparing motors on peak rather than continuous horsepower, buying a deck too short for the longest stride in the house, ignoring ceiling height and the clearance behind the belt, and not measuring the delivery route. Close behind are too little weight-capacity headroom, unpriced subscription costs, and a warranty with no labour cover.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I avoid when buying a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Avoid machines that only quote peak horsepower, decks shorter than your stride needs at your fastest pace, a weight capacity that only just covers the heaviest user, and warranties that cover parts but not labour. Also avoid ordering before you have measured the room, the ceiling and every doorway and stair turn on the way in.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it a mistake to buy a folding treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not if you will genuinely fold it. Folding is valuable in a room that has to serve another purpose. But folding frames are usually less rigid than fixed ones at the same price, the hinge is a wear point, and a folded machine still stands five to six feet tall. If it will stay down permanently, a non-folding machine is usually the better buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much ceiling height do I need for a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Add your height, the deck height of the machine and at least 12 inches of head clearance. Deck heights range from about 5 inches on a walking pad to 12 inches or more on a cushioned treadmill, so a 6-foot runner on a typical machine can need a ceiling of around 7 foot 10. Incline raises the front further.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a treadmill without trying it first?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many people do, and it usually works out, but it makes the return policy the real test. Before ordering, confirm the return window, whether it still applies once assembled, who pays for collection and whether there is a restocking fee. If you can, try a comparable deck and console at a store or gym at the speeds you will actually use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a treadmill mat on carpet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always. On bare carpet a treadmill draws fibre and dust into the motor compartment through its air intake, sits less stably, and leaves permanent crush marks. A proper treadmill mat, larger than the machine's footprint, addresses all three. On hard floors a mat is more often optional, though it still protects the floor.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes After Delivery</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Choosing well is most of the job. These four happen in the first week and undo a surprising amount of it.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not registering the warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Some manufacturers require registration within a set period, and almost all make a claim easier with it. Register on the day of delivery, keep the receipt and the serial number together, and photograph the label on the frame. It takes ten minutes and protects the terms you paid for.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the initial checks after assembly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A new belt usually needs tension and tracking checked after the first few hours of use as it beds in. Our guides on belt tension and centring the belt cover the checks, and the manual rules on the exact procedure. Running for months on a belt that has drifted is how edges fray and decks wear unevenly.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not levelling the machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill that rocks, even slightly, loads the frame unevenly and tends to pull the belt to one side. Use the levelling feet on the rear, check with a spirit level across the deck, and recheck after the first week. A mat on carpet makes this easier rather than harder.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the safety key in</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The safety key stops the belt if you fall and stops the machine starting without it. Clip it to your clothing every session, remove it afterwards, and store it out of reach of children. It only protects anyone if it is used every time.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

